{# The four things somebody checks before opening a search, then the shape of
   the trip.

   The price leads, because it is the one figure the visitor came for and the
   only one that moves. It is a "from" price and reads as one: the cheapest
   single economy seat on any upcoming date, which is what the strip below
   shows the whole of.

   The time in the air is the average and not the fastest -- see
   RouteRepository::summary(). The distance is the shortest of the airport pairs
   on the route, which is the one that is a fact about the geography rather than
   about which airport a carrier happens to use. #}
{% include 'partials/facts-grid.html.twig' with {
    'facts': [
        {
            'icon': 'fa-tag',
            'value': '$' ~ place.cheapest|round(0, 'floor'),
            'label': 'Cheapest fare'
        },
        { 'icon': 'fa-clock', 'value': duration(place.typical), 'label': 'Time in the air' },
        { 'icon': 'fa-ruler', 'value': place.km|number_format ~ ' km', 'label': 'Distance' },
        {
            'icon': 'fa-plane',
            'value': place.carriers,
            'label': place.carriers > 1 ? 'Airlines' : 'Airline'
        }
    ]
} only %}

{# The same trip the other way, which is where a reader of this page most
   often goes next. Above the map rather than at the foot of the block: it
   belongs with the price and the duration, which are the figures somebody is
   comparing when they think of the trip back.

   Absent where the pair cannot be flown home nonstop, which is a question
   RouteController::backAgain() asks rather than assumes.

   `??`, because this block is rendered by tests that hand it a route and no
   return -- and by anything else that comes to reuse it. #}
{% if reverse ?? null %}
    <p class="route-back">
        <i class="fas fa-left-right route-back__icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        Flying home?
        <a class="route-back__link" href="{{ reverse.url }}">{{ reverse.from }} to {{ reverse.to }}</a>
        from ${{ reverse.cheapest|round(0, 'floor') }}
    </p>
{% endif %}

{# Both ends on one map, with the flight drawn between them.

   The line is a great circle and not a straight one, because on the projection
   a map draws, straight is the wrong shape: New York to London leaves the coast
   heading north-east over Newfoundland, and a straight line sends it across the
   middle of the Atlantic. GreatCircle works out the points, and a route
   crossing the antimeridian comes back as two runs -- two lines, because joined
   into one the map draws the join straight back around the world.

   Marked at each city's own centre, the mean of its airports, which is what
   CityRepository::byCode() returns. No zoom: the frame is fitted to the pins
   and to the arc, which matters here more than anywhere else -- framed on its
   two ends alone, a route bowing a thousand kilometres north of them loses its
   top.

   Each pin carries its city's name, and green for where you leave and red for
   where you land -- the one convention nobody has to be taught. The pins used
   to read "1" and "2", which named neither place; the flat picture still cannot
   do better, because that endpoint takes a single character on a pin, but the
   live map has no such limit. #}
{% include 'partials/map.html.twig' with {
    'markers': [
        {
            'lat': place.from.latitude,
            'lon': place.from.longitude,
            'colour': '0EB600',
            'label': place.from.name
        },
        {
            'lat': place.to.latitude,
            'lon': place.to.longitude,
            'colour': 'EC3735',
            'label': place.to.name
        }
    ],
    'paths': place.path,
    'label': 'Map of the route from ' ~ place.from.name ~ ' to ' ~ place.to.name
        ~ ', with the flight path drawn between them'
} only %}

{# Kept now that the pins carry names, because it is not saying the same thing
   twice to the people who need it. The live map draws its labels into a canvas,
   which a screen reader cannot read at all, and the flat picture behind
   `<noscript>` has unlabelled pins because its endpoint cannot label them. This
   is the text those two cases have, and the colour key for everyone else. #}
<ul class="place__legend">
    <li class="place__legend-item place__legend-item--from">
        <span class="place__legend-role">From</span> {{ place.from.name }}
    </li>
    <li class="place__legend-item place__legend-item--to">
        <span class="place__legend-role">To</span> {{ place.to.name }}
    </li>
</ul>
